From 1a2b86d0bbdd33a0c739ce6847d07aa0ac573a95 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 6 Sep 2026 06:22:38 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] The second unrouted command: a flag placed to finish a road

Turn 697, and everything agrees except one thing:

  flag slots: mine=6 theirs=7

Buildings, serfs, inventories, players, the RNG - all identical, and all
six flags both machines share are identical too. One machine simply has a
flag the other has never heard of.

Clicking the cursor's own square while building a road means "put a flag
here and finish the road on it", and the viewport did the first half by
calling game.build_flag() straight into the local game. My earlier sweep
missed it because it was written as interface.get_game().build_flag(),
not game.build_flag() - the audit matched the receiver instead of the
method, which is the wrong thing to match. Now swept by method name across
every UI file: this was the last one. The panel's destroy_road was already
routed, and everything in the popups goes through the Interface.

It is two commands, so both are queued, flag first. A player's commands
keep the order they were queued in, so the flag is always built before the
road that lands on it, and whether either succeeds is the simulation's
business - it answers the same on both machines, so there is nothing to
test locally first.

Also, net_log_obj_diff only walked the shorter of the two arrays, so the
extra flag was never named - "mine=6 theirs=7" and not one word about
which flag. Slots past the common length are now dumped and marked ONLY
ON THIS MACHINE.

And the notice: [waiting for the other player] was shown whenever the
tick budget was zero, which lockstep does for a frame or two at almost
every turn boundary - hence the flicker. It now needs a quarter second of
continuous waiting before it says anything.

